{< Result: a value that is either a success or an error payload.

   THE DENOTATION OF THE `Error` EFFECT (proposal 047 §2, 027.4). `Error` and
   `Result` are the two halves of one monad layer, related by Filinski's pair:
   `Error.raise(x)` REFLECTS `err(x)` into direct-style code, and `Error.reify`
   REIFIES a computation back into a `Result`. `Result.reflect` below is the
   first half written out; it is what makes the two an isomorphism rather than
   a one-way try/catch — reify a computation, manipulate the `Result` as
   ordinary data, reflect it back into effectful code.

   WHY NOT `Option`. `Option` drops the payload, and for an error the payload
   IS the answer the caller wants: `KB.loaded` raises `load_failed(diagnostics)`
   and a checker's whole job is to report those diagnostics.

   PARAMETER NAMES ARE FIXED BY `Monad`, not chosen. `Monad[M[T]]` requires the
   carrier's member to be `T`, so the SUCCESS type is `T` and the payload is
   `E` — the spelling `monad.anthill`'s header already uses. `Result[E = E]` in
   the `provides` writes down which member the carrier is partially applied at;
   MEASURED, that argument is INERT at dispatch (the bare `Result` that
   `option.anthill` writes dispatches identically, and even a genuinely fixed
   `Result[E = Boom]` still admits a receiver at another payload), so it is
   documentation of the reading, not an enforcement of it. The short-circuit is
   in the `match` bodies below and nowhere else. >}
enum anthill.prelude.Result
  import anthill.prelude.{Monad, Error}

  sort E = ?                                         -- the error payload type
  sort T = ?                                         -- the success type — the Monad carrier's member

  entity ok(value: T)                                -- success
  entity err(error: E)                               -- failure, carrying the payload

  -- Result is a Monad over its SUCCESS type. Op-value bindings (not a bare
  -- `fact Monad[Result]`) for the same reason Option gives: `Monad.pure` is
  -- result-determined — it has no Result argument to dispatch on — so each
  -- witness op is named explicitly in the fact.
  -- `P` is the payload throughout and `A` / `B` are the values, so each of these
  -- reads against its `Monad` counterpart (`pure[A](a: A) -> M[A]`) with the one
  -- extra parameter being the one the partially-applied carrier fixes.
  operation resultPure[P, A](a: A) -> Result[E = P, T = A] = ok(a)

  operation resultFlatMap[P, A, B, EffP](m: Result[E = P, T = A], f: (A) -> Result[E = P, T = B] @ {EffP}) -> Result[E = P, T = B] effects {EffP} =
    match m
      case ok(x)  -> f(x)
      case err(e) -> err(e)

  operation resultMap[P, A, B, EffP](m: Result[E = P, T = A], f: (A) -> B @ {EffP}) -> Result[E = P, T = B] effects {EffP} =
    match m
      case ok(x)  -> ok(f(x))
      case err(e) -> err(e)

  -- The reflect direction: put a Result BACK into the effect channel. `ok`
  -- yields its value, `err` raises its payload — so the row gains `Error[P]`,
  -- the payload type read off the Result itself rather than asserted.
  operation reflect[P, A](r: Result[E = P, T = A]) -> A effects Error[P] =
    match r
      case ok(v)  -> v
      case err(e) -> Error.raise(e)

  provides Monad[M = Result[E = E], pure = resultPure, flatMap = resultFlatMap, map = resultMap]
end
